/**
 * Rewrites Contact.Phone into the format the new dialer accepts: digits only,
 * with an optional leading '+'.
 */
public inherited sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {

    // Every character that is NOT a digit or '+'. '+' is a literal inside a
    // character class, so it needs no escaping.
    private static final String NOT_DIALER_SAFE = '[^0-9+]';

    /**
     * Normalises the Phone field of the given contacts and saves them with a
     * single partial-success DML call.
     *
     * @param contactIds Ids of the contacts to process (callers pass up to 200).
     * @return Map from the Id of each contact that could NOT be saved to the
     *         first error message reported for that contact; empty when every
     *         save succeeded. Contacts without a phone (null or blank) are
     *         left untouched and never appear in the map.
     */
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> unsaved = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return unsaved;
        }

        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();

        // One bulk query for all Ids; no SOQL inside the loop.
        for (Contact contact : [
            SELECT Id, Phone
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id IN :contactIds
        ]) {
            // "Has a phone": treat null and empty string as no phone.
            if (String.isEmpty(contact.Phone)) {
                continue;
            }

            String normalized = contact.Phone.replaceAll(NOT_DIALER_SAFE, '');

            // Send the record through DML only when the value actually
            // changes: an update with identical values is still a full DML
            // (re-fires triggers/flows, bumps LastModifiedDate) with no gain.
            if (normalized != contact.Phone) {
                contact.Phone = normalized;
                toUpdate.add(contact);
            }
        }

        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            // allOrNone = false, so one record failing validation cannot
            // block the rest of the batch.
            List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.update(toUpdate, false);

            // Database DML returns its SaveResults in the same order as the
            // input list, so results[i] corresponds to toUpdate[i]; that
            // resolves the failing record's Id without relying on
            // SaveResult.getId() for failed rows.
            for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
                if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                    unsaved.put(
                        toUpdate[i].Id,
                        results[i].getErrors()[0].getMessage() // first error
                    );
                }
            }
        }

        return unsaved;
    }
}
```

Notes on your approach — I kept it intact otherwise:

- **Preserved as specified:** one bulk SOQL over the passed Ids, a single `replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '')` per phone, one `Database.update(contacts, false)` after the loop, and a results loop that builds the Id → first-error-message map (empty when all saves succeed).
- **One deliberate refinement:** a contact whose phone is already dialer-safe is not sent through DML. An update with identical field values is still a full DML row — it re-fires triggers, flows, and bumps `LastModifiedDate` — and since this runs in up-to-200 chunks (likely re-run across the whole org), skipping no-ops keeps re-runs idempotent. If you literally want every phone-having contact saved, just delete the `if (normalized != contact.Phone)` guard.
- **Plus-sign placement:** per your rule I keep a `+` anywhere in the string and only remove other characters. If the dialer actually requires the `+` to be *leading only* (e.g. `020+7946` would still be rejected today), say the word and I'll tighten the regex to preserve only a leading plus.
- **Fully strippable phones:** a phone like `unknown` becomes blank after stripping, which follows "remove every character that is not a digit or +". If blank should be left as-is instead, that's a one-line change.
- The class is `inherited sharing` so it honours the caller's sharing context; at ≤200 rows a single query and a single DML statement are comfortably within limits, so no chunking is needed inside the method.

